# 44. The first thing to build at kickoff

Ignore ERC-8183 initially.

Ignore the marketplace.

Ignore x402 initially.

Ignore ENS.

The first engineering target should be exactly this:

```text
existing WorkerKit run
        ↓
dstack TEE
        ↓
event chain
        ↓
artifact
        ↓
AttestedWorkReceiptV1
        ↓
TEE-derived secp256k1 signature
        ↓
fresh dstack attestation
        ↓
external independent verifier
        ↓
VERIFIED
```

Once that works, **everything else becomes composition**.

Then:

```text
+ ERC-8004
```

Then:

```text
+ ERC-8183
```

Then the fun thing:

```text
+ ERC-7710 lease
```

Then:

```text
+ x402
```

Then expose all of it through the Moltwork board.

That sequence keeps the difficult, genuinely differentiated primitive at the center instead of getting distracted by marketplace UI.

The core technical thesis I'd use for ETHOnline is therefore **“proof of work, not proof-of-compute”**: proving which agent/runtime/policy performed a real economic job, what it produced, what authority it had, what it cost, and how it settled. ERC-8004 explicitly anticipates TEE validation, ERC-8183 gives you the commerce lifecycle, ERC-7710 gives you bounded autonomous authority, and dstack gives you the execution root of trust. ([Ethereum Improvement Proposals][10])

The strongest immediate move is to **freeze `mw` as the pre-hackathon baseline and write the protocol/test vectors now, then make `AttestedWorkReceiptV1 + dstack verifier` the first event-period implementation.** That gives you one very sharp target instead of twenty integrations.

ETHOnline's partner requirements are still changing—Ledger and some others currently say “prize details coming soon.” ([ETHGlobal][19]) I can monitor that and flag any newly published prize that this architecture can hit without distorting Moltwork.

